  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1116 AM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nDespite high pressure ridge close by to the west, neutral to \nweakly cyclonic and cool (inversion top temps still -4c to -6c) \nnortherly flow aloft is keeping lake clouds over northern and far\nnortheast/eastern Wisconsin across Door County and near Lake \nMichigan, with even a few light showers, sprinkles noted recently\nover Door County. Drying, high pressure finally win out, leading \nto skies becoming mostly clear tonight into Sunday. One of the\ncolder nights of the season for most tonight with teens central \nto north-central, and 20s elsewhere, except right near the \nlakeshore. Warming trend begins on Sunday, with highs a nudge \nabove normal in the upper 40s to lower 50s. \n\nDry conditions with additional warming continues through middle \nof next week as amplified upper pattern keeps ridging locked in \nover mid CONUS. On Thursday, mid-level trough moving onshore over \nwest coast this weekend swings across northern plains and western \nGreat Lakes. Main surface low passes to north of Lake Superior but\na surface trough should bring gusty winds and some showers to the\nstate. Not a great signal for SW winds to reach anything more \nthan 35 mph (chances 10-40%), but still could be breezy. Friday \nstarts off dry, with warm/moist advection bringing 20-40% chance \nof showers back late in the day. Better signal for widespread \nsteady rain holds off until Friday night or Saturday. Decent \nagreement that with stronger low, increasing SW flow aloft and \npush of well above normal deeper moisture (PWATs), next Saturday \nis looking wet, with chances for rain now up to 60-70%. Cooler air\nlacking though, so even with the dynamic system, chances of snow \nare limited (<20% of 1\" of snow at LNL), with better chances \nfarther west on the colder side of the system.\n\nTemperatures...Highs by early next week will be solidly back into\nthe low to mid 50s. Average highs at the warmest now are in the \nmid 40s. Lows will still be seasonably cool through Wednesday \nmorning. However, with the system moving through on Thursday and a\nstronger low approaching for next weekend, highs and lows will \nsurge well above average late next week. Nighttime lows in the \nupper 30s to upper 40s will be more typical of what the highs \nshould be. Subject to cloud cover and shower coverage, based on \npattern and 90th percentile from NBM, highs on Thursday through \nSaturday could reach the lower 60s.",
